
EXPERIMENT 4: CONTAINERIZE AN APPLICATION USING DOCKER

STEP 1: CHECK DOCKER INSTALLATION

docker --version
docker info

STEP 2: CREATE PROJECT FOLDER

mkdir ASDD-Experiment4
cd ASDD-Experiment4

STEP 3: CREATE HTML APPLICATION

notepad index.html

Paste the following code into Notepad:

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Docker Experiment</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Hello from Docker!</h1>
    <p>My first containerized application.</p>
</body>
</html>

Save and close Notepad.

STEP 4: CREATE DOCKERFILE

notepad Dockerfile

Paste the following code:

FROM nginx:alpine
COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html
EXPOSE 80

Save and close Notepad.

IMPORTANT:
If the file is saved as Dockerfile.txt, rename it using:

ren Dockerfile.txt Dockerfile

Verify the files:

dir

You should see Dockerfile and index.html.

STEP 5: BUILD DOCKER IMAGE

docker build -t asdd-web-app .

STEP 6: VERIFY DOCKER IMAGE

docker images

Expected:
Repository: asdd-web-app
Tag: latest

STEP 7: RUN THE CONTAINER

docker run -d -p 8080:80 --name asdd-container asdd-web-app

This command starts the container in the background and maps host port 8080 to container port 80.

STEP 8: VERIFY RUNNING CONTAINER

docker ps

Expected:
The container asdd-container should appear in the NAMES column.

STEP 9: TEST APPLICATION

Open a browser and visit:

http://localhost:8080

Expected output:

Hello from Docker!
My first containerized application.

STEP 10: VIEW CONTAINER LOGS

docker logs asdd-container

STEP 11: STOP THE CONTAINER

docker stop asdd-container

STEP 12: START THE CONTAINER AGAIN

docker start asdd-container

Verify:

docker ps

STEP 13: STOP AND REMOVE THE CONTAINER

docker stop asdd-container
docker rm asdd-container

If the container is already stopped, docker stop may report that it is not running. You can still remove it with:

docker rm asdd-container

STEP 14: VERIFY THE IMAGE STILL EXISTS

docker images

The asdd-web-app image should still be listed.
